WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:01.820
- [Narrator] Wild fires are ravaging

00:00:01.820 --> 00:00:03.560
the Western US this year,

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:05.920
and in California, inspectors like these

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.250
are going door to door,

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:09.320
making sure homeowners have a perimeter

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:12.430
of defensible space between
the house and the forest.

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:13.532
- What kind of dog do you have?

00:00:13.532 --> 00:00:15.476
- A pit bull.
- Okay.

00:00:15.476 --> 00:00:16.940
- I cleared a lot of this out

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:19.284
but I still have the back section to do.

00:00:19.284 --> 00:00:20.990
- When we do our inspection,
we'll start at the roof

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:23.210
and work down and then out from the house.

00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:27.160
Any branches that are
overhanging the house,

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:28.050
we want those cleared back 10 feet.

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:28.883
- Okay.

00:00:28.883 --> 00:00:31.880
- You as a homeowner might
think I'm doing the right thing,

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:33.620
and I've cleared a little of this

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:36.340
or I've cleared some
plants away from my house,

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:38.200
but the defensible space inspectors

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:40.300
have been trained to
notice maybe something

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:41.650
a homeowner hasn't.

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:43.960
- [Narrator] This is a new
approach for California,

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:45.320
which is desperate to find ways

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:47.480
to reduce the impact of wild fires

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:50.340
after a record fire year in 2020.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:52.780
But as fires once again
rage in parts of the state

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:55.860
and the Western US, why are
many experts questioning

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:57.560
whether California is prepared

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:00.070
for another explosive fire season?

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:02.220
In 2020, wild fires burned

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:04.110
a record-breaking 10 million acres

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:05.610
in the Western US.

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:08.180
Nearly half of that was in California.

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:10.820
Last year saw five of
the six largest fires

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:12.420
in modern state history

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:15.110
with more than 10,000 structures destroyed

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:18.320
and 33 deaths, according to CAL FIRE.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:21.270
This year, conditions,
fueled by climate change,

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:24.140
are even worst according
to climate scientists.

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:27.260
Much of the region is facing
extreme drought conditions

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:30.077
and heatwaves have set
record temperatures.

00:01:30.077 --> 00:01:32.990
(truck zooming)

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:35.750
Experts say fighting fires is not enough.

00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:36.922
Michael Wara directs the Climate

00:01:36.922 --> 00:01:39.790
and Energy Policy Program at Stanford.

00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:42.130
He says a new strategy for preventive work

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:45.750
and land management is needed
to reduce wildfire risk.

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:50.750
- What we're seeing is a sea
change, in California at least,

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:54.030
in the way that this
problem is thought about.

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:56.060
- [Narrator] That's why
California's fire agency,

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:57.860
CAL FIRE, has this team

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:00.800
of defensible space
inspectors going door to door

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:02.500
in regions like Butte County

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:06.530
where the campfire destroyed
the town of Paradise in 2018.

00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:08.520
Small home improvements
can be the difference

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:10.740
between a house burning or not.

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:12.770
- Your chimney over here,

00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:14.239
you want 10 feet away from that.

00:02:14.239 --> 00:02:15.900
- Yeah, makes sense.

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:17.310
- Just make sure that's kept clear.

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:18.520
- [Narrator] CAL FIRE says they've done

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:22.120
around 3,200 inspections
in the county so far

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:25.239
and plan to get to 6,000
by the end of the year.

00:02:25.239 --> 00:02:28.410
But for many, it may be
too little, too late.

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:30.960
In California, more than
11 million people live

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:33.000
in areas that border wild lands,

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:35.053
according to the US Forest Service.

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:38.700
Focusing on preventive work is challenging

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:40.370
when firefighters have their hands full

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:42.833
fighting active blazes across the state.

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:47.320
Fire agencies like CAL
FIRE are already stretched

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:49.200
and grappling with firefighter burnout

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:51.083
from a now year-long season.

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:54.690
John Gaddie is a fire
captain in Butte County.

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:56.650
- We're asking our men
and women of CAL FIRE,

00:02:56.650 --> 00:02:58.320
not only of CAL FIRE, but our federal

00:02:58.320 --> 00:02:59.740
and local government counterparts

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:02.630
to work longer, harder summers,

00:03:02.630 --> 00:03:04.080
and it's taking their toll on them,

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:05.890
not only mentally but physically.

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:07.930
- [Narrator] The federal
government controls the majority

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:09.380
of California's forests

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:12.470
but is struggling to recruit
and retain firefighters,

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:14.610
according to the US Forest Service.

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:16.990
Grassroots Wildland Firefighters,

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:18.620
an advocacy organization,

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:21.580
estimates 20% of permanent
firefighting positions

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:24.010
at the Forest Service are unfilled.

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:26.340
CAL FIRE says they added almost 1,400

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:29.680
new seasonal firefighter
positions this year.

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:32.100
However, the state says
they relied heavily

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:33.730
on inmate fire crews,

00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:35.220
which have been dramatically reduced

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:37.030
because of recent prison reform

00:03:37.030 --> 00:03:40.150
and the early release of many
prisoners during the pandemic.

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:44.250
- We're down 50 plus or minus percent

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:45.870
in our fire crews statewide,

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:49.520
which at the end of the day
on some of these larger fires,

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:51.860
the men and women, the fire crews

00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:53.780
and the fire engines are the ones

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:55.550
that actually get the line put in

00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:58.480
and contain and control these fires.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:01.410
So if we have one large fire,

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:03.490
pretty much our crews or the fire crews

00:04:03.490 --> 00:04:05.493
of CAL FIRE are utilized.

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:08.860
- [Narrator] More resources
may be on the way.

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:10.480
President Biden recently pledged

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:12.510
to hire more federal firefighters

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:15.740
and increase the pay to
at least $15 an hour.

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:17.410
But some experts think the government

00:04:17.410 --> 00:04:18.520
should be spending as much

00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:21.690
on wildfire risk
reduction as firefighting.

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:23.660
In his California budget proposal,

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:25.930
Governor Newsom called for $2 billion

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:27.820
to reduce wildfire threats,

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:30.333
much of it earmarked for work like this.

00:04:30.333 --> 00:04:32.170
(chainsaw buzzing)

00:04:32.170 --> 00:04:33.360
Cutting new fire breaks

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:35.480
and thinning overgrown forests,

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:37.630
as well as removing dead and dying trees

00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:39.931
that become tinder for massive blazes.

00:04:39.931 --> 00:04:41.870
(wood crunching)

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:43.880
Fuel reduction crews like this one,

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:46.260
working outside of Oroville, California

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:48.010
are on the job year round.

00:04:48.010 --> 00:04:49.800
And the urgency is clear.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:50.820
As the crew worked,

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:54.139
a fast-moving wild fire
started just miles away.

00:04:54.139 --> 00:04:56.010
(chainsaw buzzing)

00:04:56.010 --> 00:04:58.240
Wara is encouraged by the
progress in this state

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:00.150
but he says the National Forest Service

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:01.880
is not doing enough.

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:03.114
- We should be spending as much

00:05:03.114 --> 00:05:05.900
to reduce the risk of wild fire

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:08.760
as we are to fight it when it occurs.

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:09.850
And the federal government

00:05:09.850 --> 00:05:12.120
is not moving in the right direction.

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:13.310
- [Narrator] The Forest Service reduced

00:05:13.310 --> 00:05:15.080
their hazardous fuel reduction budget

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:18.230
by more than $200 million in 2021,

00:05:18.230 --> 00:05:20.320
and while the proposed 2022 budget

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:22.150
represents a steep increase,

00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:24.730
it is still below pre-pandemic levels.

00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:27.120
- Budgets are policy as
far as I'm concerned.

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:28.440
And they tell you what's important

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:29.670
and what's not important.

00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:34.610
And the Forest Service proposed

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:38.210
a hazardous fuel reduction budget

00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:40.030
for the entire United States.

00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:42.150
It's smaller than California's budget.

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:43.960
- [Narrator] The National
Forest Service didn't respond

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:45.520
to our request for comment,

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:47.210
but in a recent Senate hearing,

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:49.040
a Forest Service official said the agency

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:50.560
hasn't had the resources

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:53.330
to carry out necessary
prescribed fire work,

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:54.163
and that they needed

00:05:54.163 --> 00:05:56.420
to boost their fuel management projects.

00:05:56.420 --> 00:05:58.900
- We have to scale up
our work by at least two

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:01.420
to four times what we're doing right now.

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:02.750
(birds tweeting)

00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:03.930
- [Narrator] As summer progresses,

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:05.330
experts say little can be done

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:07.240
to mitigate the immediate danger,

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:08.870
but fire crews are still hoping

00:06:08.870 --> 00:06:11.540
for a less exhausting
and destructive season.

00:06:11.540 --> 00:06:12.940
- Are we ready to respond right now?

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:13.865
Absolutely.

00:06:13.865 --> 00:06:16.770
I just don't wanna see that onslaught

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:20.050
and unfortunately, I
think the environment,

00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:21.670
being hotter and drier

00:06:21.670 --> 00:06:23.720
and lack of precipitation,

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:25.770
and some of the fires we've already seen,

00:06:27.970 --> 00:06:29.406
it doesn't look good.

00:06:29.406 --> 00:06:32.156
(dramatic music)

